Z||000013||Tonker||09-09-2001||10:52 PM||||So you guys don't find water gets into the air filter? That was my only real reason for avoiding that. I had assumed (yeah I know what they say  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">) some shielding would be required to keep it dry.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Boomtastic||09-09-2001||11:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tonker:<BR><B>So you guys don't find water gets into the air filter? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Washable air filters are not like paper ones - they are coated with oil that repels the water, so they won't fall apart if they get wet. Personally, I don't think the amount of water that *could* make it through is enough to worry about because there is more water in the gas you buy than gets past the air filter. A little water will burn off in the combustion cycle and you'll never notice it. (In the old days, racers used to inject cold water into their engines to create the ultimate in dense air charge.)<P>------------------<BR>Boomtastic<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mirror_md_clr.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||pontiacs4me||09-10-2001||01:13 AM||||water injection isnt used so much for creating a denser air charge, but more for preventing detonation in high compression engines on pump gas.  <BR>I was talking to one of the managers at a local parts store today, he told me about a project chevelle him, and a couple of his friends had back in the day.  It had 17:1 compression and water injection, and it ran detonation free on premium pump gas.||||reg||1
